  "narrative": "## Pathway Analysis and Biological Interpretation\n\nThe RaMP enrichment analysis identified **Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism** (KEGG map00150, FDR = 0.0, fold enrichment = 1091.7) as the dominant affected pathway, with six steroid hormones driving this signal: Dehydroepiandrosterone, Androstenedione, Testosterone, Dihydrotestosterone, Estrone, and Estradiol. This pathway also appeared as the top hit across multiple databases — SMPDB's \"Androgen and Estrogen Metabolism\" and Reactome's \"Metabolism of steroid hormones\" — confirming the robustness of the finding.\n\n**Key driver compounds.** Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA, C01227) occupies a pivotal upstream position, serving as the principal precursor for both androgens and estrogens. KEGG path analysis confirmed direct one-step connectivity from DHEA to Androstenedione (C00280), which in turn converts to Testosterone (C00535) — the major circulating androgen in males. The pathway extends further to Dihydrotestosterone (C03917), the most potent androgen receptor agonist, via a single enzymatic step. In the estrogen arm, bidirectional one-step paths connect Estrone (C00468) and Estradiol (C00951), reflecting the active interconversion catalyzed by 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenases.\n\n**Biological significance.** Systematic elevation or coordinated modulation of these six steroids suggests disruption of the gonadal steroid axis — potentially reflecting altered steroidogenic enzyme activity (e.g., CYP17A1, aromatase, or 5α-reductase) or hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal feedback dysregulation. Such a pattern is consistent with endocrine disorders, xenobiotic exposure, or pathological states affecting hormone biosynthesis. The simultaneous perturbation of both androgen and estrogen branches, rather than a single branch, points to a upstream defect at the DHEA branch point.\n\n**Non-steroidal compounds.** Caffeine (C07481), sucrose (C00089), inosine 2',3'-cyclic phosphate (C05768), N-hydroxy-4-aminobiphenyl (C20319), and 4-hydroxy-L-glutamic acid (C05947) did not achieve pathway significance in this analysis. Their association may reflect indirect or tissue-specific effects rather than core steroid metabolism reprogramming.",
